WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.067
A multipart filename describes what the
sender called a file.

2
00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:07.294
It should not decide where the application
writes it.

3
00:00:07.294 --> 00:00:10.012
Using that value as a filesystem path or

4
00:00:10.012 --> 00:00:13.239
object storage key combines display
metadata with storage authority.

5
00:00:13.239 --> 00:00:15.618
The application then has to reason about

6
00:00:15.618 --> 00:00:18.250
path separators, reserved names, control
characters, Unicode

7
00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:21.223
presentation, excessive length,
collisions, and platform differences every

8
00:00:21.223 --> 00:00:23.771
time a file is saved or retrieved.

9
00:00:23.771 --> 00:00:26.149
Remove that dependency by generating
storage identifiers

10
00:00:26.149 --> 00:00:28.951
on the server, using an appropriate random
identifier

11
00:00:28.951 --> 00:00:31.499
mechanism such as Node's random UUID
function.

12
00:00:31.499 --> 00:00:35.406
Keep the original name separately only
when the product needs it.

13
00:00:35.406 --> 00:00:39.653
Bound that metadata, escape it for its
rendering context, and use a

14
00:00:39.653 --> 00:00:43.899
proper header construction library when a
download name enters an HTTP response.

15
00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:46.617
A name acceptable for display is not
thereby

16
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:49.844
acceptable as a storage address or
response header value.

17
00:00:49.844 --> 00:00:53.326
An identifier should select a new object,
while replacement should

18
00:00:53.326 --> 00:00:56.554
be a separate authorized operation on a
logical attachment.

19
00:00:56.554 --> 00:00:58.592
Use exclusive creation or appropriate
storage

20
00:00:58.592 --> 00:01:00.460
conditions to prevent accidental
overwrites.

21
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.943
If an extension is operationally useful,
derive it from the

22
00:01:03.943 --> 00:01:07.170
validated file policy rather than copying
the sender's suffix.

23
00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.973
Cleaning a filename can improve
presentation, but it

24
00:01:09.973 --> 00:01:13.200
should not carry the burden of controlling
storage destinations.

25
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.107
Server generated names make the allowed
destination much easier to review.

26
00:01:17.107 --> 00:01:19.655
For local storage, configure a trusted
root,

27
00:01:19.655 --> 00:01:22.457
create only server controlled child names,
and prevent

28
00:01:22.457 --> 00:01:25.005
untrusted actors from modifying the
directory structure.

29
00:01:25.005 --> 00:01:27.723
Resolve destinations within that root and
account for

30
00:01:27.723 --> 00:01:30.611
links or filesystem behavior that could
redirect writes.

31
00:01:30.611 --> 00:01:32.309
Restrictive permissions protect temporary
directories

32
00:01:32.309 --> 00:01:34.178
as well as permanent storage.

33
00:01:34.178 --> 00:01:37.999
For object storage, the server chooses the
bucket or container and

34
00:01:37.999 --> 00:01:41.991
key, and records the relationship to the
authorized tenant and resource.

35
00:01:41.991 --> 00:01:45.473
An opaque key reduces name collisions and
guessability; it does

36
00:01:45.473 --> 00:01:49.380
not replace access control on the object
or its download route.

